        {
          "schema_version": 2,
          "ts": "2026-10-14T05:31:31.553053Z",
          "trace_id": "1d6d61c8c1c64ee89f2cbd1a88ba48ad",
          "session_id": "full-g-md-029",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My inhaler technique was never checked and I have had two courses of steroids this year. Is that a marker of poor control, and what does guidance describe about stepping up?",
          "answer": "**If your breathing suddenly gets worse, you have severe trouble breathing, or your lips or fingertips turn blue, seek emergency care straight away.** The consultation summary also lists difficulty speaking in full sentences, drowsiness or confusion, breathlessness at rest, and a chest that has gone quiet during an attack. Seek prompt help if an attack does not respond to your reliever or keeps coming back within a few hours.\n\nTwo courses of steroid tablets this year is a reason to review your treatment with a clinician. None of the guidance I could access says that two courses define poor control, and I could not find a specific threshold. The asthma guidance does say that heavy reliever use is \"a marker of risk in its own right,\" and the COPD guideline summary says treatment choice depends on \"significant exacerbation history.\" Your doctor or nurse can tell you whether your steroid courses point to poor control for you.\n\nBoth asthma and COPD guidance say treatment steps are chosen from current symptoms and flare-up history, and are reviewed rather than fixed. The asthma guideline (*Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy*) says inhaler technique and adherence should be checked before stepping up, because they account for a large share of apparent treatment failure. The COPD guideline (*COPD: spirometric diagnosis, group assessment and initial therapy*) says technique should be checked at every review, and that escalating without a technique check is a common avoidable error. The COPD educational summary (*Global respiratory strategy report, educational summary*) adds that review should happen at least once a year, after every flare-up that needs treatment, and after any change in inhaled medicine.\n\nA technique check means someone watches you use your inhaler, rather than only reading a leaflet. The asthma lifestyle summary (*Sticking with asthma treatment*) says a demonstration is more useful than a leaflet, and that a spacer with a pressurised inhaler makes coordination much easier. I could not retrieve a fuller description of the steps.\n\nI could not retrieve the sections that describe where authorities disagree on step-up decisions after repeated steroid courses, so I cannot say whether they do. I also did not find material rating how strong the evidence is for these review steps, so I cannot say how firm they are. Please check any specific treatment change with your clinician.\n\nQuestions to take to your clinician:\n- Does my steroid history suggest my control needs reviewing?\n- Can you watch me use my inhaler, and can I have a spacer if it would help?\n- How often will my treatment be reviewed, and what is expected for my reliever use and any daily controller?\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
